Title: WE'RE FROM THE FUTURE
Credit: Episode One
Author: "The Package"
Draft date: Production Draft — v3.0 (Locked)
Contact: Season One, Episode One

====

# TEASER — "ORDINARY"

FADE IN:

EXT. CEDAR ROW — LATE AFTERNOON

An ordinary street. Low single-storey houses from the fifties. Chain-link. A basketball hoop with no net. A recycling bin that has been out two days too long.

Long lens. Locked off. Nothing happens.

A car passes, left to right.

Somewhere, a screen door.

In the fourth house, a kitchen light comes on.

We hold. Four seconds longer than is comfortable.

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — CONTINUOUS

Warm. Lived-in. Mismatched chairs. A good knife and a bad pan.

LAUREN WARD (28) stands at the stove doing three things at once and none of them well. She is entirely happy about this.

MILES WARD (30) leans against the counter with a stack of mail, sorting.

MILES
This one's for the Petersens again.

LAUREN
Keep it.

MILES
It's from a hospital.

LAUREN
Then definitely keep it.

He sets it on a pile. She reaches past him for the salt; he shifts out of her way without looking up. Fifteen years of choreography in half a second.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Taste this.

MILES
No.

LAUREN
Taste it.

MILES
I watched you make it.

She holds up the spoon. He tastes it. Considers. Considers longer than necessary.

MILES (CONT'D)
It's good.

LAUREN
You hesitated.

MILES
I was chewing.

LAUREN
It's soup.

Beat. He laughs — a short exhale through the nose. She goes back to the stove, pleased with herself.

She crosses to the fridge, takes a pen off a magnet, and adds a line to a notepad stuck there.

INSERT — THE NOTEPAD

Her handwriting. *bins — Thurs.* Below it she starts to write a time.

7

Her pen lifts. Comes back down, angled — the beginning of a cross-stroke through the number.

It stops. Hovers. A quarter of an inch away.

She lifts the pen. Leaves the seven bare.

BACK TO SCENE

She caps the pen without ceremony and puts it back on the magnet.

CHIRP.

A single low electronic chirp from the ceiling. The smoke detector.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
There it is.

MILES
I'll get a battery.

LAUREN
You've said that.

MILES
And I've meant it every time.

INT. WARD HOUSE — HALLWAY — CONTINUOUS

Miles carries the mail through. The hallway is narrow, warm, papered in something inoffensive.

On the wall: four bare picture hooks. Evenly spaced. No frames. Just nails, and four small pale rectangles where nothing has faded.

Lauren follows him out, drying her hands.

LAUREN
I'm going to fill those.

MILES
Sure.

He does not slow down.

LAUREN
I've got the one of the lake. I could get it printed.

MILES (O.S.)
Sure.

She stops. Looks at the nails a moment.

Then follows him.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — EVENING

Two lamps and a television. An old film, black and white, the sound low. They are folded onto the couch in the way of people who have worked out the geometry.

On screen, a WOMAN in a coat stands on a platform.

MILES
(under his breath, to no one)
She was never on the train.

Three seconds later, ON THE TELEVISION, a DETECTIVE says something to precisely that effect.

Lauren turns her head.

LAUREN
How.

MILES
Lucky guess.

LAUREN
That's four.

MILES
Four what.

LAUREN
You know what.

MILES
I genuinely don't.

LAUREN
Mm.

She lets it go. She always lets it go. That is also part of the choreography.

She reaches for her phone, thumbs the camera open, and lifts it toward him.

Miles turns his face away — instantly, smoothly, into her shoulder. It is played entirely as a joke.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
One picture.

MILES
Absolutely not.

LAUREN
You are a very strange man.

MILES
(muffled, into her shoulder)
I photograph poorly.

She lowers the phone. She is smiling.

The smile stays a beat past where it should. Then she puts the phone face-down on the arm of the couch.

Outside, distantly: music, and people laughing. A party two doors down.

LAUREN
They're doing it again.

MILES
It's Friday.

LAUREN
We should go over sometime.

MILES
Sure.

LAUREN
That's three "sure"s tonight and you've meant zero of them.

MILES
I mean all of them equally.

She looks toward the window, and the noise, and the ordinary street.

LAUREN
(dry)
We're not from around here.

A quarter-second. Miles looks at her.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
(of the neighbours)
Them. They're from Ohio. She tells everybody.

MILES
I know what you meant.

He doesn't look away for another beat. Then he does.

INT. WARD HOUSE — FRONT DOOR — LATER

Miles turns the deadbolt. Solid. Done.

He takes two steps away.

He comes back and turns it again. Tests it with his palm.

Then he leans in to the narrow window beside the door and looks down the street. Left. Then right. Then left again, longer.

Lauren is in the hall behind him, a glass of water in her hand.

She doesn't say anything.

She has watched him do this a thousand times.

She turns back toward the kitchen. As she passes the wall:

A CALENDAR. Cheap, from a hardware store.

One date is circled in blue pen.

No note. No name. Nothing written in the square at all.

She goes past it without looking at it. Which is its own kind of looking.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

The film plays. Lauren settles back down. Miles comes in and sits.

For a moment, nothing. Just a warm room and two people in it.

DING-DONG.

The doorbell.

Neither of them moves.

LAUREN
Did you order something?

MILES
No.

A long pause. Longer than the question deserves.

MILES (CONT'D)
Did you?

LAUREN
No.

He gets up.

INT. WARD HOUSE — FRONT DOOR — CONTINUOUS

Miles unlocks. Opens the door on the chain first. Then all the way.

The porch is empty. The street is empty. The mat is dark with rain.

On the mat, dead centre, squared perfectly to the edge of the step:

A SMALL BROWN BOX.

CUT TO BLACK.

A low sustained tone — one note, unresolved, more felt than heard.

MAIN TITLE resolves out of the black, letter by letter, thin and wide-tracked:

**WE'RE FROM THE FUTURE**

The tone stops. The card holds three seconds in silence. Then it is gone.

====

# ACT ONE — "THE BOX"

INT. WARD HOUSE — ENTRY — NIGHT

The box sits on the hall floor where Miles set it down. Neither of them has come closer than four feet.

It is small. Brown. Unbranded. Utterly ordinary.

MILES
Don't touch it.

Lauren is already crouched beside it, palm flat on the top.

LAUREN
It's dry.

MILES
What?

LAUREN
It rained. The mat's soaked through. This is dry.

She runs a thumb along an edge.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
And the corners are perfect. Miles, look at the corners.

He crouches. Looks.

Every corner is sharp. No crush, no scuff, no thumbprint. It does not look like a thing that has been anywhere.

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — MOMENTS LATER

They have put it under the light, on the table, on a tea towel — an absurd, careful, domestic instinct.

Lauren turns it. On one face: a label.

INSERT — THE LABEL

A shipping label. White. Printed. Correct in every visual respect and wrong in every specific one.

Their address. No sender. No carrier name, no logo, no service class.

There is a barcode. Or rather: there is the *shape* of a barcode. The bars do not repeat the way bars repeat.

At the bottom, in blue ballpoint, in a small neat hand:

*1 of 1*

And a six-digit consignment number.

The seven in it is crossed.

BACK TO SCENE

LAUREN
There's no company on it.

MILES
There's a barcode.

LAUREN
There's a barcode *shape*.

She leans closer. Her eyes move down to the handwritten line.

And she stops.

Everything about her stops.

MILES
What?

LAUREN
Nothing.

MILES
Lauren.

LAUREN
It's nothing.

She puts her hand flat over the bottom of the label.

Which is not nothing.

Miles watches her hand. He doesn't ask again.

MILES
We put it in the car. We drive out to the county road. We leave it.

LAUREN
And then what.

MILES
Then it's somewhere else.

LAUREN
Then it's somewhere else and we come home to this house. Which somebody stood in front of.

He has nothing.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
If someone knows where we live, then the box is the least interesting thing that happened tonight.

A long moment. The refrigerator cycles on. Rain starts again, lightly, against the window over the sink.

MILES
Okay.

LAUREN
Okay?

MILES
We open it. And then we talk. Properly. Not the version where you ask and I say it's fine.

LAUREN
(a beat, softer)
Okay.

She gets a knife from the block. The good one.

The blade goes through the tape.

It is a small sound and it is enormous.

She cuts the second seam. The third.

The refrigerator hums. Rain. A car two streets over.

CHIRP.

They both flinch — hard. And then, immediately, both laugh at themselves, badly, with too much air in it.

MILES
Battery.

LAUREN
Tomorrow.

She opens the flaps.

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — CONTINUOUS

Inside: grey foam, cut to shape. No packing slip. No manual. No plastic.

Set into the foam, flat:

THE LEAF.

A single pane. Roughly the size of a large book. Thin as a magazine. Matte, dark, the colour of wet slate.

No frame. No bezel. No port, no seam, no screw, no button, no brand, no serial, no mark of any kind on either face.

It does not look futuristic. It looks *finished*.

Neither of them says anything for a while.

MILES
(quietly)
Don't.

Lauren lifts it out.

LAUREN
It's heavy.

MILES
It's a screen.

LAUREN
No — Miles, hold it.

She holds it out to him. He takes it, two hands, the way you take something borrowed.

It is far heavier than it should be. And—

He adjusts his grip.

MILES
It's moving.

LAUREN
What?

MILES
The weight's moving. Inside it.

He tilts it, slowly. The mass shifts across the pane, unhurried, a beat behind the motion. Like a spirit level. Like something settling.

He sets it down flat on the table. Carefully.

He takes his hand off it, then puts two fingers back on.

MILES (CONT'D)
It's cold.

Lauren slides her hand underneath, palm up, against the other face.

LAUREN
It's warm.

They look at each other.

Miles turns the pane over. Sets it down again.

Lauren touches the top face. Then the underside.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
It's warm on the bottom.

MILES
It was warm on the bottom before.

LAUREN
Miles. That's a different side.

She sits down. Slowly. Not because she is frightened — because she is *thinking*, and thinking is what she does with her whole body.

She looks at the pane.

The kitchen is in it. Dimly. The lamp, the cabinets, the shape of her own shoulder.

She raises her hand.

The hand in the pane raises.

Late.

She lowers it. Raises it again, watching.

Down. Up. And the reflection comes with her — a half-beat behind, every time, smooth and unbothered.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles.

MILES
I see it.

LAUREN
Look at my hand.

MILES
I see it, Lauren.

His voice has changed. Flattened.

She looks up at him.

Miles has stopped moving. Completely. Both hands flat on the table. He is not looking at her. He is looking at the pane the way a man looks at a name on a list.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Hey.

Nothing.

And then—

THE PANE WAKES.

Not an image. Not a menu. Not a logo.

AN EYE.

Filling the whole surface. Wet. Living. Human. The pupil contracts fractionally in the lamplight.

It blinks. Once.

DARK.

Less than a second. Gone as if it had never been.

The refrigerator hums.

Lauren stares at the dead pane. Then, slowly, up at her husband.

Miles has gone white. Absolutely white, from the collar up.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles?

He doesn't answer.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
You know what that is.

He doesn't answer.

SMASH TO BLACK.

====

# ACT TWO — "SIGNAL"

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — CONTINUOUS

Miles moves. Fast. He has the box in one hand and he is reaching for the pane with the other.

MILES
It's a display unit. It's a demo. Somebody's testing a—

LAUREN
Miles.

MILES
—there's a cell in it, a printed cell, they do that now, it holds a charge for—

LAUREN
*Miles.*

He stops, one hand over the pane.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
You didn't jump.

MILES
What?

LAUREN
It turned on. In our kitchen. In the middle of the night. And you didn't jump.

Beat.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
I jumped. The smoke alarm made me jump. That thing opened an eye at me and you stood there like you were reading a bill.

MILES
I'm putting it in the car.

LAUREN
No.

MILES
Lauren—

LAUREN
No.

She does not raise her voice. She puts one hand flat on the pane and leaves it there and looks at him.

He could move her. They both know he could move her. He doesn't.

MILES
We agreed.

LAUREN
We didn't agree to this.

MILES
We agreed that if anything ever came near us we don't pick it up, we don't turn it over, we don't keep it in the *house*—

LAUREN
That is not what we agreed.

MILES
It is *exactly*—

LAUREN
We agreed we wouldn't go looking!

That does land. That is louder.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
(back down, immediately)
Nobody ever said what to do when it comes to the door.

The refrigerator. The rain.

MILES
(quiet)
Don't be found.

LAUREN
Don't be found. Don't be recorded. Don't go back. Don't answer. Don't send anything forward.

She says it flat and fast, without effort, the way you say something you have said ten thousand times.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
I know them, Miles. I say them at three in the morning. In order. Every night. Have done for six years.

MILES
(something crosses him)
Six years.

LAUREN
Six years and four months and we have not said one word about any of it out loud in this house and I have *kept* that. I kept it because you asked me to.

She takes her hand off the pane.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
So don't tell me what we agreed.

She picks the leaf up. Both hands, careful, like a tray.

MILES
Where are you going.

LAUREN
I'm putting it on the table where we can see it.

MILES
Don't put it down.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

She puts it down.

Flat on the coffee table, dead centre. She sits back on the couch. Miles stays standing in the doorway, which is exactly between Lauren and the front hall, and he does not appear to know he has done that.

The television is still on, sound low. The old film. Nobody is watching it.

Nothing happens.

Nothing keeps happening.

Lauren pulls a cushion into her lap. Miles shifts his weight.

MILES
This is—

THE LEAF WAKES.

No sound. No boot. No logo. The light simply arrives, and it is on their faces.

Miles goes silent mid-word.

ON THE PANE:

A FACE, in darkness. Lit from somewhere low and moving, so that the light shifts across it slowly, as if from water, or a fire, or a screen we can't see.

It is LAUREN'S FACE.

The same age. The same eyes. The same mouth.

Her hair is down and unstyled. Something dark and high-necked at her throat.

There is a fine pale scar under her left eye, running back along the cheekbone. Old. Well healed.

She is absolutely still.

Lauren does not move. Neither does Miles.

DOUBLE (V.O.)
Lauren.

It is Lauren's voice.

Flatter. Slower. No smile in it at all. And she does not take a breath before she speaks.

Lauren's hand has gone to her own mouth without her deciding to do it.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
I know. Take a second.

LAUREN
(barely)
What is this.

DOUBLE
You have a burn on the inside of your left wrist. Two inches, curved. You got it on a stove that isn't in this house.

Lauren's other hand closes over her left wrist.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
You still say them at three in the morning.

Beat.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
In order.

Lauren makes a sound. Not a word.

Miles takes one step into the room.

MILES
Who is this.

The face does not answer him.

But she is looking at him.

She has been looking at him this entire time.

DOUBLE
(to Lauren)
You've been here too long.

LAUREN
What?

DOUBLE
It was supposed to be short. Somewhere to be, for a while.

(beat)

Six years isn't a while. It's started to hold.

MILES
Hold—

DOUBLE
(to Lauren, over him)
There's a correction. I don't know how far out it is. I don't think it's far.

LAUREN
A correction of what?

DOUBLE
Of the part where you stayed.

Silence.

Miles crosses the room. Fast. He gets one hand on the pane—

—and stops. Because his hand is on it and nothing changes. The face keeps talking. He might as well have put his hand on a wall.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
Lauren, listen. There's a thing you need to not do, and I need you to hear it from me, because you won't hear it from—

LAUREN
How do you have my face?

For the first time, the face hesitates.

It is a small thing. Half a second. But it is the first human beat she has had, and it is horrible, because it means she is deciding what to say.

DOUBLE
Because I—

STATIC.

The pane tears open into FOOTAGE:

INSERT — THE FOOTAGE

Three seconds. Handheld, degraded, the wrong shape — narrower, older, like something recovered rather than filmed.

NIGHT. Outdoors. Hard rain in a hard light.

MILES and LAUREN. Older. Not much. Enough.

They are moving fast — not running, *escorting*, one of them holding the other up. Miles has blood in his hairline and down the side of his face.

Behind them, badly out of focus, high up: a SIGN. Sodium yellow. Half a word legible.

Somebody off-frame is shouting. A name. It is not "Miles" and it is not "Lauren."

BACK TO SCENE

BLACK.

The pane is dead. The room is dark except for the television, still playing its old film to nobody.

Neither of them moves.

LAUREN
There was a sign.

MILES
There wasn't.

LAUREN
Behind us. Yellow. Up high.

MILES
Lauren, it was three seconds of—

LAUREN
There was a *sign*, Miles.

She turns and looks at him properly for the first time since it started.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
And you saw it. Because you stopped breathing.

He doesn't answer.

He doesn't answer for a long time.

Rain on the window. The film ends and the credits start rolling, silently, on a television nobody is watching.

CHIRP.

CUT TO BLACK — and total silence.

====

# ACT THREE — "RULES WE BROKE"

INT. WARD HOUSE — VARIOUS — NIGHT

No music. Almost no sound. Just a man working.

Miles slides the pane face-down into the bottom drawer of the sideboard and shuts it.

He stands there. Then he reaches out and touches the brass drawer pull.

It is cold.

He takes his hand back.

—

He puts it in the camping cooler. Clips the lid. Stands back.

Then, slowly, he puts his palm on the lid of the cooler.

It is warm.

—

He goes to the breaker panel in the hall closet and throws the main.

The house dies. Refrigerator, television, the lamp in the hall, all of it, gone at once.

He comes back to the living room in the dark.

The pane is lying on the coffee table where Lauren has put it back.

It is glowing.

EXT. CEDAR ROW — NIGHT

Wide. The street.

Every house has its lights on except one.

A dog barks twice, somewhere. A television flickers blue through a window. Two doors down, the party is still going: laughter, a car door, somebody calling somebody else an idiot with enormous affection.

The world is completely, insultingly fine.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — NIGHT

Power back on. One lamp.

Lauren sits in the armchair, facing the coffee table. She has put Miles's canvas jacket over the pane.

She has not moved in a while.

Miles comes in and stands.

MILES
I know what it is.

She doesn't look up.

MILES (CONT'D)
Not who sent it. I don't know that. But I know what it is.

LAUREN
You've seen one.

MILES
I've held one.

LAUREN
When?

He doesn't answer.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Okay.

She is not being generous. She is filing it.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Then here's the one I actually want.

She looks up.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Why me? It knows my wrist. It knows what I do at three in the morning. It's got my *face*, Miles. Why does it want me and not you?

Miles opens his mouth.

Nothing comes out.

Lauren watches his hands go flat on the back of the couch.

She has seen that before too.

She gets up.

MILES
Where are you—

She crosses to the table, lifts the jacket, and takes the label out from under the pane where she left it.

She reads it.

Then she walks out of the room.

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — CONTINUOUS

Lauren stops in front of the wall calendar.

The circled date. Blue pen. Nothing written in it.

She looks down at the label in her hand.

She looks up at the calendar.

She stands there a long time.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

She comes back in with the label held up between two fingers.

LAUREN
What happens on the fourteenth.

MILES
Nothing.

LAUREN
It's on the box, Miles. It's the consignment date. And it's the one day of the year you circle on a calendar and don't write anything in.

Silence.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Six years I've watched you circle that. I never asked. I decided it was — I decided it was somebody's birthday, and that you'd tell me when you could.

She lowers the label.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
So tell me when you can.

Miles sits down on the arm of the couch. It takes him a moment to do it.

MILES
Once a year.

LAUREN
Once a year what.

MILES
I send nine words.

The room is very quiet.

LAUREN
You send.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
Forward.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
That's five.

MILES
I know what it is.

LAUREN
Say it.

MILES
That's clause five.

LAUREN
(quietly)
How many times.

MILES
Six.

Lauren turns away from him. All the way around, hands on top of her head, looking at nothing.

LAUREN
What are the nine words.

MILES
Lauren—

LAUREN
What are the nine words, Miles.

Beat.

MILES
"We're still here. We're okay. Don't come. I'm sorry."

She turns back.

LAUREN
Who's "I'm sorry" for?

He doesn't answer.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Who is it for?

MILES
There's somebody who thinks we didn't make it.

LAUREN
Who.

MILES
Lauren.

LAUREN
*Who?*

He can't. He physically cannot get it out. His mouth works and nothing comes.

And Lauren — watching him fail — understands something and goes absolutely still.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Oh.

She looks down at the label in her hand. At the crossed seven. At the date.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles.

(beat)

You didn't get a package.

MILES
Don't.

LAUREN
You got an answer.

MILES
Lauren—

LAUREN
YOU PUT OUR ADDRESS IN THE AIR ONCE A YEAR FOR SIX YEARS AND LET ME THINK I WAS SAFE!

It rips out of her. It is the loudest thing in this house in six years and it is over in two seconds and the silence afterward is enormous.

Miles has not moved.

MILES
(low)
It's nine words. There's no address in it. There's no—

LAUREN
There's a *when*.

That stops him.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Every year. Same day. Same time, I'd bet. You didn't tell them where we are. You told them we're *regular*.

Miles puts his hand over his mouth and holds it there.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
They didn't find us. You waved.

Long silence.

MILES
They told us nobody would come looking.

LAUREN
They told us a lot of things.

MILES
They said the whole point — the entire point, Lauren, the reason for all of it, the reason we don't have photographs and I check a street I have checked eleven thousand times — was that if we did it properly, nobody would ever have to come.

LAUREN
And you did it properly.

MILES
(a wreck)
I did it properly for three hundred and sixty-four days a year.

LAUREN
Yeah.

She sits down on the coffee table, right beside the covered pane, because her legs have stopped being interested.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
I'm not angry that you lied.

MILES
You are.

LAUREN
I'm a *little* angry that you lied.

Almost — almost — the ghost of the two people from the beginning of the evening.

Then it's gone.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
You didn't protect me, Miles.

(beat)

You just made sure that when it came, I'd be the one who was surprised.

He has no answer to that. There isn't one.

The lamp flickers — no. Not flickers. The room has just got *colder*. You can see it: her breath isn't visible, but her arms come in.

She gets up, takes a grey cardigan off the back of the chair, and pulls it on.

MILES
It does that.

LAUREN
Does what.

MILES
When it's about to.

She looks at him. Six years of not asking, and now every single answer is a new injury.

MILES (CONT'D)
Lauren.

LAUREN
Don't.

MILES
Lauren.

She stops.

He comes over. He doesn't touch her. He gets close enough to.

MILES (CONT'D)
I have been so scared for so long that I forgot it was a thing that could end.

She doesn't look at him. But she doesn't move away.

MILES (CONT'D)
I thought if I could just — hold it — for long enough—

He raises his hand. Puts it on her arm, just above the elbow.

She lets him.

Ten seconds. Two people almost, almost getting back.

LIGHT.

The pane, under the jacket, comes on so bright that the canvas glows from underneath.

Miles pulls the jacket off.

ON THE PANE:

No face. Black field. Four words in thin white type, wide-tracked, plain:

**DO NOT GO BACK**

Lauren stares at it.

LAUREN
...Okay. That's — that's what you've been saying. That's what you've been saying all night. Stay put. Do nothing. That's *good*, isn't it—

She turns to him.

Miles has taken a step back from the table.

Not a flinch. A *retreat*. He has put distance between himself and four words on a screen and he is looking at them like they are a person in the room.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles?

MILES
That's not advice.

LAUREN
What?

MILES
That's not somebody warning us. That's not — Lauren, that's *word for word*.

She looks at the screen. Then at him. And then she gets it, and it takes all the blood out of her too.

MILES (CONT'D)
That's clause three.

LAUREN
(whisper)
Somebody over there is reading us our own Terms.

MILES
Somebody over there *has* them.

Beneath the four words, small, precise, unhurried, a number appears:

**12:00**

It becomes **11:59**.

Then **11:58**.

LAUREN
(very quietly)
Down to what.

CUT TO BLACK.

====

# ACT FOUR — "THE HOUSE IS LISTENING"

INT. WARD HOUSE — KITCHEN — NIGHT

The overhead light on. Everything visible. Two people working.

There is no panic in this room, and that is the frightening part.

Lauren has a pad and is writing a list in fast, legible capitals. Miles is at the back door.

MILES
Back door, alley, left, and you're on Weir in ninety seconds. Front door, you're on Cedar, and Cedar only goes two ways.

LAUREN
Car?

MILES
Car's on Cedar.

LAUREN
So the car's decorative.

MILES
The car's a place somebody would wait.

He says it without drama, the way you'd say the milk's off.

He goes to the hall closet, reaches up past the coats, and takes down two canvas duffels.

They are grey with dust across the top.

Lauren stares at them.

LAUREN
How long have those been up there?

MILES
Since we moved in.

LAUREN
Six years.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
You packed one for me.

MILES
I packed two.

She takes hers. Unzips it. Cash in a band. A phone still in its box. Socks. A folding knife. A coat she does not remember owning.

And, at the bottom, face-down: a small photograph.

She turns it over.

We do not see it.

She looks at it for a long moment.

LAUREN
We're not allowed to have this.

MILES
I know.

She puts it in her back pocket.

Her phone buzzes on the counter.

She picks it up. Reads it. Then stops.

INSERT — THE PHONE

A message. From no one. Blank body.

Received: **3:14 AM**.

The clock at the top of the screen reads **11:26 PM**.

BACK TO SCENE

She turns the phone around and shows him. He looks at it for two full seconds.

MILES
Okay.

LAUREN
"Okay"?

MILES
I'm putting it in the drawer.

He takes the phone, opens a drawer, puts it in, and closes it.

It is such a calm, useless, human thing to do that Lauren almost laughs.

Behind them: the microwave clock.

**11:26**

It becomes **11:25**.

Then it sits there. And then, without hurrying, it goes back to **11:25** again — the same minute twice, four seconds of a night happening a second time.

They both see it.

Neither of them says one word about it.

Lauren picks up her list. Her hand is not entirely steady. She keeps writing.

LAUREN
Money, coats, the label. What else.

MILES
Nothing else. That's the whole point of the bags.

She goes to reach for her phone in the drawer — stops herself — takes it out anyway.

She opens the camera roll to check something.

And her thumb stops moving.

INSERT — THE PHONE

The most recent photograph.

The living room of this house. Tonight — the lamp is in the position it is in tonight, the jacket is on the arm of the couch.

MILES and LAUREN, asleep, folded together on the couch.

The photograph is taken from across the room. From the doorway.

BACK TO SCENE

Lauren holds the phone out to him without a word.

Miles looks.

MILES (CONT'D)
When.

LAUREN
Tonight. That's the lamp. That's your jacket.

MILES
We didn't sleep tonight.

LAUREN
No.

They look at each other.

CHIRP.

The smoke detector, above them. Same as it has been all week.

And then, from the living room:

CHIRP.

The same tone. The same length. A half-beat late.

Miles goes to the doorway. Looks through into the dark living room.

The pane is lying face-up on the coffee table. It is black. The countdown is not showing. It is doing nothing at all.

CHIRP.

It comes from the pane, while he is standing there looking at it, and there is nothing on it to make a sound.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

Lauren comes in behind him.

LAUREN
It won't do anything for you.

MILES
What?

LAUREN
You put your whole hand on it and it kept talking. I walk past it, it wakes up.

She crouches down in front of the coffee table. Level with the pane.

MILES
Lauren.

LAUREN
I know.

MILES
That's four. Don't answer it. That's *four*, Lauren, that's the one you've never—

LAUREN
And five's yours. So we're even.

That lands. He stops.

She looks at the black pane.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
(to it)
If you want us to do something, show me where.

Nothing.

Nothing.

And then the pane comes on — not to a face. To a photograph. A still. Held.

INSERT — THE STILL

Night. Rain. A low, long building beside a state highway. Two storeys, external walkway, twelve doors. Sodium light.

A tall sign on a pole, yellow, with one letter dead:

**BLU RIDG MOT L**

Held. Four seconds.

Gone. Black.

BACK TO SCENE

Lauren is on her feet.

LAUREN
That's the sign. Miles, that's the sign from the — that's the sign.

He is not looking at the pane. He is looking at the middle distance, and he has gone somewhere else entirely.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles. What is it?

MILES
That's where we came in.

Beat.

LAUREN
...What?

MILES
Room eleven. Second floor, end of the walkway. That's the crossing.

He says it flatly. It is the single largest thing he has said all night and he says it like a street name.

LAUREN
I don't remember that.

MILES
No.

LAUREN
Miles, I don't remember *any* of that.

MILES
I know.

He finally looks at her.

MILES (CONT'D)
And I have never once known what to do with that.

Lauren stands in the middle of her own living room and feels the floor go.

She looks at the dead pane.

LAUREN
It told us not to go back.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
And then it showed us where to go.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
So one of those is a lie.

MILES
Yes.

LAUREN
Which one?

MILES
I don't know.

THE PANE COMES ON.

The FACE again. Closer than before. Filling more of the frame.

The double is looking out at them, steady, tired, kind.

DOUBLE
Lauren.

Lauren does not answer her.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
I'm going to say something, and it's going to be unkind, and I'm sorry, and I'd do it again.

Beat.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
Count backwards for me. I'll be here at one.

Lauren's face comes apart.

It is not a scream and it is not tears. It is the specific and terrible expression of a person hearing, in their own voice, out of a machine, a sentence that has only ever existed in one room in the world, and that room does not exist yet.

She sits down on the floor.

Her hand goes to the pendant at her throat and closes around it. A reflex much older than her marriage.

LAUREN
(barely audible)
My mother said that.

MILES
(off her)
Lauren—

LAUREN
My mother said that to me. Every night. Nobody knows that.

She looks up at the screen.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
I have never said that out loud. Not once. Not here. Not to him.

The double's face does something small and very sad.

DOUBLE
I know.

(beat)

She said it to me too.

(beat)

Didn't she, ———

The sound is not there.

There is a name. Her mouth makes it. It is two syllables and it is *not* "Lauren" — and where the sound should be there is a clean, absolute, half-second absence. Not a bleep. Not static. A hole.

Miles's head snaps around.

He heard it. Or heard the shape of it.

MILES
(very quietly)
What did you just call her.

The double does not answer him.

She never has.

But she is looking at him.

LAUREN
(getting up, unsteady)
What am I?

DOUBLE
You're the one who—

BLACK.

Every light in the house goes out at once.

The refrigerator stops. The microwave clock dies. The whole building drops into darkness in a single beat.

Lauren freezes.

Miles goes straight to the window and pulls the curtain aside two inches.

EXT. CEDAR ROW — CONTINUOUS — MILES' POV

The street.

Every porch light on. Every window lit. The party two doors down, still going, warm and yellow and loud.

Every house on the street has power.

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

Behind Miles, on the coffee table, the pane is glowing steadily.

It is the only light in the house.

Miles looks out along the street, left — right — and then back to the left. To the same spot he checks every night of his life.

There is a FIGURE standing at the edge of the streetlight's throw.

Not close. Not moving.

Miles turns his head fully to look at it directly.

There is nobody there.

He does not move for a long moment.

Then, from the front hall, very clearly, in a completely silent house:

The deadbolt turns.

One full revolution.

CUT TO BLACK.

====

# ACT FIVE — "SOMEONE FOUND US"

INT. WARD HOUSE — LIVING ROOM — CONTINUOUS

Dark. The pane is the only light in the world and it is the wrong colour for a home.

Neither of them screams. Neither of them runs.

Miles crosses the room, takes the armchair by the back, and drags it — quietly, without hurry — into the mouth of the hallway. Not a barricade. An obstacle. A thing to trip on in the dark.

Lauren shoulders her bag, picks up the pane, wraps it in the cardigan, and puts it in the bag. Then she takes the fire iron from the hearth and puts it on the coffee table where either of them can reach it.

They move around each other in the dark like people who have done a drill.

Because they have.

MILES
Back door.

LAUREN
No.

MILES
Lauren, the alley's clear, you go left and you're—

LAUREN
No.

MILES
Please.

LAUREN
I'm not doing the part where you decide for me twice in one night.

That stops him dead.

MILES
That's not what this is.

LAUREN
That's exactly what this is. You've done it for six years. It's the only thing you're actually good at.

Beat.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
(gentler, and worse)
Miles. We're doing this one together or we're not doing it.

He looks at her in the light off the bag.

MILES
Okay.

LAUREN
Okay.

She checks the pane through the fabric. The number is showing through the weave.

**01:20**

**01:19**

MILES
There's something you need to know before that gets to zero.

LAUREN
Then say it fast.

MILES
Nobody comes over.

LAUREN
What?

MILES
It's not against the rules. It's — Lauren, it isn't a rule, it's a *fact*. People don't cross. Things don't cross.

LAUREN
We crossed.

MILES
We came *out*. That's different. That's — that's falling. Anyone can fall.

(beat)

Coming the other way costs something. It costs more than a person has.

LAUREN
Meaning what.

MILES
Meaning nothing comes through whole.

He is not trying to frighten her. He is telling her a load-bearing fact about the world, and he is terrified.

MILES (CONT'D)
So whatever is out there — whatever it used to be — it paid everything it had to stand on our step. And it did that on purpose. And it did it *tonight*.

The number in the bag:

**00:41**

Lauren stares at him in the dark.

LAUREN
Miles, I have to ask you something and I need you to not manage me.

MILES
Okay.

LAUREN
That message.

(beat)

Did it come from me?

Miles opens his mouth.

He does not lie to her. He genuinely, visibly, does not lie to her.

He also does not say anything.

Three seconds.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Okay.

That is the answer, and she takes it standing up.

LIGHT.

Inside the bag, through the cardigan, the pane brightens.

Lauren pulls it out.

INSERT — THE PANE

The double. Closer than she has ever been. Her face nearly fills it.

She is looking straight out — and her eyes are not on Lauren.

They are on Miles. Where they have been all night.

BACK TO SCENE

LAUREN
Who *are* you?

And, for the first time in the entire episode, the double looks away from Miles.

She looks at Lauren.

DOUBLE
I'm the one who left when they told me to.

Beat.

DOUBLE (CONT'D)
Don't.

The number, small, beneath her face:

**00:04**

**00:03**

**00:02**

**00:01**

**00:00**

Nothing happens.

The house is silent. The pane holds her face, perfectly still, waiting with them.

And then:

KNOCK. KNOCK. KNOCK.

A pause.

KNOCK. KNOCK.

Three. Then two.

It is not a stranger's knock. It has a *shape*. It is a knock with a private meaning, the kind you and one other person in the world agree on.

Miles's head comes up.

And everything in his face falls apart at once — not fear. Not fear at all.

Recognition.

LAUREN
(watching him)
Miles.

Nothing.

LAUREN (CONT'D)
Miles, what.

And from outside — through the door, muffled, unhurried, and warm —

VOICE (O.S.)
It's me.

Miles closes his eyes.

WIDE — THE LIVING ROOM

The dark house. The two of them, side by side at last, shoulder against shoulder, lit from below by a pane of glass that should not exist.

Behind them, deep in the frame, the front door.

Its frosted panel is a soft rectangle of streetlight.

Something on the other side of it moves — a shift of weight, a change in the light, a shape that is a person and is not quite the right size for a person.

It does not resolve.

The deadbolt is already open.

CUT TO BLACK.

Silence.

Then, in the black, thin and wide-tracked:

**WE'RE FROM THE FUTURE**

**END OF EPISODE ONE**

====

# TAG — POST-CREDITS

INT. A PALE ROOM — NO TIME GIVEN

White. Not clean-white — *lit*-white, from everywhere, so that nothing casts a shadow and no corner of the room can be located.

A FIGURE stands at a surface. We see them from behind, from the shoulders down. We will not see their face. We will not see their hands clearly.

They are holding a flat, unlit sheet, roughly the size and thickness of the pane.

INSERT — THE SHEET

Two records, side by side. No photographs. No names we can read — the designations are long strings that do not resolve as language.

The first record's status field reads:

**LOST**

BACK TO SCENE

The figure touches it, once.

INSERT — THE SHEET

**LOCATED**

BACK TO SCENE

The figure moves to the second record.

INSERT — THE SHEET

The second record's status field already reads:

**LOCATED**

Beside it, a date.

It is nineteen years old.

BACK TO SCENE

The figure stops.

Just stops. The only human thing that happens in this room.

SYSTEM VOICE (V.O.)
(flat, female, pleasant, unhurried)
Retrieval is already in progress.

BLACK.

FADE OUT.

**END**
